<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nguyễn Du</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1766–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nguyen Du</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nguyễn Du was a Vietnamese poet and official, regarded as the greatest poet in the Vietnamese language. After serving the Lê court, he took office under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a> and led an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1813. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-kieu">Truyện Kiều</a>, or The Tale of Kieu, a verse novel of more than three thousand lines written in chữ Nôm, is the most celebrated work of Vietnamese literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nguyen-du.jpg" alt="Nguyễn Du" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vienvo03 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C6%B0%E1%BB%A3ng_%C4%91%C3%A0i_c%E1%BB%A5_Nguy%E1%BB%85n_Du.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
